@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@section Welcome
@nodedescription Welcome to libspacetime!

libspacetime is a cross-platform library for date and time operating
across multiple planets, while matching the current standards and
specifications for date and time functions as best as possible.

Currently, only Mars is supported. Other planets may be supported in
the future. (Of course, this may be difficult, as years become longer
the farther the planets are from the sun. But maybe other systems,
being relative to a planet's moons or systems of other orbital bodies
could be considered).

@node Philosophy
@section Philosophy

When initially creating this library, there was the question of "why?".
After all, it was 2026, and no standard for timekeeping beyond Earth
had been created.

That year, the Artemis II mission was a success. The later goals of the
Artemis mission is to setup a moon base to then be used to bring humans
to Mars.

It was the perfect time to start preparing computers to assist humanity
with timekeeping on Mars. The Darian Calendar system, has been notable
enough to have its own Wikipedia page, and there are various
implementations in other programming languages, but not in C.

Again, although the Darian Calendar had not been declared "official",
it seemed to have the most potential.

Thomas Gangale, who proposed the Darian Calendar, also proposed a
system for Jupiter and Titan. Although those would definitely be years
away, and likely work differently due to the length of orbit, there was
thought being put into humans tracking time in space. For this reason,
the library was named "libspacetime" instead of "libdarian" to allow
for future expansion.

In the more immediate future, the reasoning for a C implementation of
the Darian Calendar would allow for integration with other parts of
a UNIX-based system, and eventually, all other kinds of software.
Language bindings could be made to work with space time at a performant
low level.

In addition, to help ease support of developers adopting the new
calendar system, the library would conform to existing practices and
APIs to make its use trivial. For C programs, it would be as if they
were calling functions directly from the C standard library, although
they are not. This allows for the "proposed" time systems to be
separate from the existing, relevant, and more important Earth systems
in the C standard library.

Timekeeping for Mars would be alongside timekeeping for Earth, and make
tracking both easy. The idea of timekeeping for Mars has not been
exclusive to hobbyists or thinkers: NASA has included a local clock for
certain locations in Mars24. With other calendar systems being
developed beyond Mars, it makes sense to lay the foundation for future
timekeeping in a way that is similar to what humans are already
familiar with.

